 # Shear-warp
-
 Also called: Shear-warp factorization, Shear-wrap
-
 ## Overview
-The Shear-warp family solves the problem of efficiently rendering volumetric data from arbitrary viewing angles by factorizing the 3D viewing transformation into a 3D shear and a 2D warp [S1, S3, S7, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from general ray-casting methods by transforming the volume into a "sheared object space" where viewing rays are parallel to a coordinate axis, allowing for efficient slice-by-slice compositing with fixed interpolation weights [S3, unofficial][S12]. Unlike splatting, it supports early ray termination via opaque pixel offsets, and unlike pure texture slicing, it handles arbitrary rotations without requiring pre-computed texture maps for every angle [S3, S7, unofficial].
-
+The Shear-warp family solves the problem of efficient volume rendering from arbitrary viewing angles by factorizing the viewing transformation matrix into a 3D shear and a 2D warp [S1][S2, S4, S5, unofficial]. Unlike general ray-casting methods that perform per-pixel traversal, Shear-warp transforms the volume into a "sheared object space" where viewing rays are parallel to a coordinate axis, allowing for efficient slice-by-slice compositing [S12]. It sets itself apart from its siblings by leveraging the associative nature of the compositing operator to enable early ray termination and empty space skipping, which pure object-order methods like [Splatting](23-splatting.md) could not perform efficiently in 1994 [S4, unofficial]. It differs from [Texture slicing](25-texture-slicing.md) by explicitly separating the volume traversal (shear) from the final projection (warp), avoiding the need for complex texture coordinate generation per sample [S4, S8, unofficial].
 ## Shared principles
-The Shear-warp family relies on the factorization of the viewing transformation matrix $M_{view}$ into three components: a permutation $P$, a shear $S$, and a 2D warp $M_{warp2D}$ [S2, S4, S7, unofficial].
-
+The Shear-warp family shares the fundamental mechanism of decomposing the viewing transformation $M_{view}$ into three components: a permutation $P$, a shear $S$, and a 2D warp $M_{warp2D}$ [S2, S5, S8, unofficial].
 $$ M_{view} = M_{warp2D} \cdot S \cdot P $$
-
-1.  **Permutation ($P$):** The algorithm determines the principal viewing axis by finding the coordinate axis (X, Y, or Z) most aligned with the view direction vector $v_o$ [S12][S4, unofficial]. A permutation matrix $P$ is constructed to align this axis with the Z-axis of an intermediate coordinate system [S2, unofficial][S12]. If the view is along X or Y, the voxel coordinates are reordered by transposing the axes [S3, unofficial]. To avoid re-encoding data when the view direction changes, three Run-Length Encoded (RLE) structures are typically pre-computed, one for each principal axis [S3, S4, unofficial].
-2.  **Shear ($S$):** The volume slices (perpendicular to the principal axis) are sheared so that viewing rays become parallel to the Z-axis in the sheared object space [S2, unofficial][S12]. For parallel projection, the shear coefficients $s_x, s_y$ are derived from the view direction $v_o = [v_{o,x}, v_{o,y}, v_{o,z}]^T$:
-    $$ s_x = -\frac{v_{o,x}}{v_{o,z}}, \quad s_y = -\frac{v_{o,y}}{v_{o,z}} $$
